lso is very good for  the corn in particular the multifidus  muscles which are the very small muscles  that run up the spine  and their job they're rich in muscle  spindles those are sensors that detect  and predict movement  and what they do is they prepare for  movement so if you're stepping down a  step  it's the multifidus muscle that gets you  ready gets you braced for that  slight drop that you're expecting this  is a really good thing to  train if you want to prevent injury and  at the same time improve your  ability to move healthily and  impressively the key here then  is not to perform this quickly but to  hold  each position for a couple of seconds  get the arm out nice and straight in the  leg  and this time we're just going to do 10  on each side and it's a bit of a nice  break  push up is a difficult one like i say a  lot of people can't brace their core  throughout them  let alone muster enough peck strength to  push themselves up  this makes it difficult to do enough  volume to trigger the kind of response  we want so the solution  is to do the incline push-up which i  think is even easier than doing it on  the knees  if you're still learning and the great  thing is it's extremely adaptable  so what you're going to do is you're  going to grab a chair a bed a bench  anything you like put your hands on it  and then just perform a push-up  like so from there again it's important  to keep your core as straight as  possible so you want your body to be in  a nice straight line  and then you're going to try and perform  30 of those in a row again  with a minimum rest in between  and once again if it's too easy then all  you need to do  is lower the height and if it's too  difficult  increase the height and to do that you  can push yourself up against a wall even  so  barely any weight on there at all but  just practicing that movement and  practicing  keeping the core nice and straight and  then we're doing skater hops and all  you're going to do for this is jump from  side to side  tap behind jump to one side to tap  behind so you're landing on the outside  leg and then the inside one is coming  across  and tapping behind that one and the  reason this is useful  first of all this is a movement in the  frontal plane the frontal plane meaning  side to side  as opposed to the sagittal plane meaning  forwards and back or up and down  that's missing in so many workouts  especially beginner programs  and it's something again that we have to  do all the time just like that rotation  so we want it  in there in some form to build that  stable base  and at the same time it teaches more  coordination it's very basic movement  and if you can perform that it's going  to help you with all kinds of other  things  that you can add onto later on down the  road  so i said you didn't need any specialist  equipment and then here i am hanging  from a rope but bear with me  because this same exercise can be  performed with any kind of setup  whether it's a towel draped over pull up  bar whether it's a towel draped around a  door handle or whether it's a towel  draped around a bed  so a towel but to be honest you can also  use a bed sheet  or just literally anything you can hang  from and pull yourself from trees are  great  and basically what we're going to be  doing here is a bodyweight row so you're  going to hang on to the rope  lean backwards and then you're going to  pull yourself up and down  as you go and again the further back you  lean the more difficult it will be and  the more upright you are the easier it  is so it's really adaptable to any  difficulty level  this is a perfect exercise because it's  the perfect counter  to the push-up so whereas the push-up is  an anti-extension movement this is an  anti-flexion movement you're  practicing keeping your body in that  rigid position but now it's when there's  something pulling on the front of you  very similar to when we were doing the  squats but holding something light in  front of us  and this is also a pulling movement  which we really need to balance out all  of those pushing movements many  beginner workouts only include pushing  movements anything that's entirely on  the ground is gonna have a hard time  challenging those pulling movements  which can lead to an imbalance where  you're hunched over because all of your  front muscles are pulling you forwards  and you haven't built those back muscles  to pull you  back again seeing as we're hunched over  anyways at the desk which causes  kyphosis and other issues  that's not ideal that said if you don't  have access to a pull-up bar or a tree  or a rope or a  bed then don't worry about it too much  because we are just starting out  and trying to write the perfectly  balanced program can actually end up  standing in the way of progress  just get doing something and then work  on improving those missing areas later  on  if you have to admit this exercise it's  not the end of the world  next up we're going to do some plank and  plank is something that's very  misunderstood by a lot of people  there's been a lot of narrative on the  web at the moment about plank being  useless and not particularly helpful  because we already  spend so long in a anti-extension  position  and because it's not that challenging  etc etc but actually for someone who  really lacks the core strength necessary  just to keep their body rigid  this is a really good way to build up  that basic strength there that's going  to help to perform better quality  push-ups  and all kinds of other exercises at the  same time it can help to prevent injury  because there's the endurance aspect to  it again it's about not just being  strong enough in the core  but being able to maintain that strength  throughout a busy tiring day  and still not injure yourself so  beforehand  all you're going to do put your arms on  the floor  and hold your body like so the most  important thing is that you're  braced in the core and that means that  your waist isn't sagging like that  it needs to be in the nice straight line  position and that's what we're working  on  you can hold this for 30 seconds to  begin with that's absolutely fantastic  if that's too easy to get up to a minute  if it's too hard drop onto your knees  and still try and hold that same  position  for 30 seconds and this is what i'm  going to share a really useful tip for  all of these exercises and that's  the mechanical drop set if you can do  something but you can't do it  to the volume or for the duration that  i'm prescribing here  then what you need to do is perform the  most difficult version for as long as  you can  then switch immediately to the easier  version so for instance  if you can do plank for 10 seconds but  you can't do it for 20 seconds or 30  seconds  and all you need to do is to get into  that position to start with  and as soon as that starts getting  tiring just drop your knees and continue  you can do the exact same thing with the  squats for instance if you can do squats  with a light kettlebell  perform those for as long as you can and  as soon as that becomes difficult throw  the  kettlebell to one side and carry on  performing those squats  and gradually you'll do more and more of  the difficult ones and fewer and fewer  of the easy ones mechanical drop sets  massively underutilized tool  that are fantastic for all kinds of  things essentially lets you  train max strength and control but at  the same time to go for those higher rep  ranges  that trigger hypertrophy and improved  endurance so it's the best of both  worlds  and this is a circuit meaning that we're  going to complete the entire thing a  total of three times that's the aim  you can just do twice to begin with  that's absolutely fine 30 second break  between each exercise and one minute  break  in between each lap of the circuit or  two if you really need it  so you found that useful interesting  guys if you did then please leave a like  and share around that helps me out  immensely  hit the subscribe button for more like  this and the bell button if you want to  be notified when i post new videos  if you like functional training and you  want to learn more about it then you  probably know already that i have a new  book out called functional training  and beyond which released very recently  in  the us and will be releasing soon across  europe and the rest of the world  please check out the description down  below i massively appreciate all your  support so far  guys the comments on facebook were so  like  just generous and kind and i couldn't  believe the response i got with so many  people entering the contest  three people have now been selected to  win the signed copy  so stay tuned i'll be in touch and we'll  get those sent out to you  as soon as possible to everyone else  thank you so much for entering again  thank you so much for the support  i appreciate it more than you know i  think i have the the best community on  youtube but i'm sure everyone says that  but  i think it might actually be true in my  case i'm extremely lucky  um so yeah thanks for watching this one  thanks for all the support  up until now and stay tuned for more  coming very soon  thanks time for watching and bye for now
 

